<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Henry, Prince of Nassau-Saarbrücken</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1718">1718</a>–<a href="/years/1768">1768</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Henry was Prince of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a> from 1741. With his architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-joachim-stengel">Friedrich Joachim Stengel</a> he rebuilt Saarbrücken as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> residence, and he promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining and ironworks in the Saar, laying foundations for the region's later industry. His work was continued by his son Louis, the last ruling prince.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-henry-prince-of-nassau-saarbrucken.jpg" alt="William Henry, Prince of Nassau-Saarbrücken" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Bergholz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WilhelmHeinr007-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William Henry was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usingen">Usingen</a> on 6 March 1718, the fifth son of William Henry of Nassau-Usingen, who died weeks before his birth, and Princess Charlotte Amalia of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a>. His mother ruled as guardian and regent until her death in 1738 and raised her sons in the Calvinist faith. He studied at the University of Strasbourg in 1730 and 1731, probably also at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, and his Grand Tour took him to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>.</p><p>His brother Charles acted as regent until William Henry came of age in 1741, when the brothers divided their inheritance: Charles took Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usingen">Usingen</a> on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and William Henry Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a> on the left bank, with about 22,000 inhabitants one of the smallest principalities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He served with his Royal-Allemand regiment in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>, sold it in 1742 to the Landgrave of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, and later fought with his own troops in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. Close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he travelled often to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and was promoted to field marshal. On 28 February 1742 he married Sophie, daughter of Count George William of Erbach, at Erbach.</p><p>At home he separated administration from justice, standardised taxes, introduced a cadastre on the Austrian model and promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> cultivation and pest control. He nationalised the mines and leased the ironworks to entrepreneurs such as Cerf Beer. His building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a>, which had suffered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> and the War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reunions">Reunions</a>, included Saarbrücken Castle, the Louis Church and the Basilica of St. John, besides hunting lodges such as Schloss Jägersberg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>William Henry died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a> on 24 July 1768. His spending on building left an immense debt to his son and successor Louis, though his buildings still dominate the city. He is seen as an example of both the possibilities and the limits of enlightened absolutism: he upheld legal reform, economic stimulus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>, yet allowed his subjects no part in government, regulated every area of life by decree and harshly suppressed social protest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Henry, Prince of Nassau-Saarbrücken” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 18th century Prince <b>William Henry</b> and his son Louis made Saarbrücken a Baroque residence, with buildings by Friedrich Joachim Stengel including the Ludwigskirche, and developed coal mining and ironworks.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau-saarbrucken">County of Nassau-Saarbrücken</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usingen">Usingen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.33,8.54&amp;year=1750" aria-label="Usingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4745 415 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Henry, Prince of Nassau-Saarbrücken"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5195 685h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5195 685h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.33,8.54&amp;year=1750">What was here in 1750? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
